T(33, "33-4", "Where Has the Night Gone?", "Explanation", [
"A hundred years ago, anyone who stepped outside on a clear night, away from the glare of a town, could see a pale river of light stretching across the sky. It was the Milky Way, the vast crowd of stars that includes our own sun. Today, a child growing up in a city may reach adulthood without seeing it once. The reason is light pollution, a problem so ordinary that most of us have stopped noticing it.",
"Light pollution is the name for artificial light that is brighter than it needs to be, or that shines where it is not wanted. Streetlamps, floodlights, shop signs and lit windows all send some of their light upwards instead of down towards the ground. Some of it bounces off the pavement, and tiny particles in the air scatter it, so that a dome of dim orange light hangs over every town. This is known as skyglow, and it can be seen for many miles. It drowns out the faint light of distant stars, so that in a brightly lit city only the very brightest can be made out.",
"For astronomers, skyglow is a serious nuisance. Telescopes need dark skies to gather faint light, so many observatories have been built on remote mountaintops, far from towns. Even there, the glow of a distant city can creep in.",
"Yet it is wildlife that suffers most. Many animals evolved in a world where night was truly dark. Baby sea turtles, having hatched on a beach, find the sea by crawling towards the brightest part of the horizon, which is normally the sky reflected over the water. Bright lights on the land behind the beach can send them the wrong way. Many migrating birds travel at night, and lit buildings can pull them off course and leave them circling until they are exhausted. Moths spend hours swirling around lamps instead of feeding.",
"Humans are not unaffected. Our bodies keep an internal clock, guided by light and darkness, that tells us when to feel sleepy. Bright light late in the evening, especially the bluish light of some lamps and screens, can make it harder to fall asleep. Streetlights shining through thin curtains can have a similar effect.",
"The encouraging news is that light pollution is one of the easiest kinds of pollution to reduce. Unlike a spilled chemical, it disappears the moment a light is switched off. Shields fitted over streetlamps direct the beam down onto the road where it is needed. Warmer-coloured bulbs are less harmful than bluish ones, and dimmers or timers can lower the brightness when few people are about. In some places, communities have created \u201cdark-sky reserves\u201d, where lighting is strictly controlled so that visitors can gaze at the stars.",
"None of this means sitting in darkness. It simply means using light more carefully: the right amount, in the right place, at the right time.",
], [
 dict(d="2b",k="short",m=1,q="What is the Milky Way, according to paragraph 1?",a="The vast crowd of stars that includes our own sun.",g="Accept: a huge group of stars / a pale river of light in the sky."),
 dict(d="2a",k="mc",m=1,q="In paragraph 2, what does \u201cscatter\u201d mean?",opts=["Collect together","Send in many directions","Make louder","Freeze"],a="B"),
 dict(d="2c",k="order",m=1,q="Number the steps from 1 to 4 to show how skyglow forms.",items=["A dome of dim orange light hangs over the town.","Streetlamps and signs send light upwards.","Light bounces off the ground and is scattered by particles in the air.","Lights are switched on in the town."],a=[4,2,3,1],g=Q2),
 dict(d="2d",k="short",m=2,q="Explain why many observatories are built on remote mountaintops. Use the text.",a="They need dark skies to collect faint light, and towns create skyglow.",g="1 mark for needing dark skies / faint light. 1 mark for the reason that towns\u2019 light spoils this (skyglow)."),
 dict(d="2b",k="find",m=1,q="Find and copy the word from paragraph 4 that describes baby sea turtles finding the sea.",a="crawling",g="Accept \u201ccrawling\u201d."),
 dict(d="2d",k="short",m=2,q="How do bright lights affect (a) baby sea turtles and (b) migrating birds? Use the text.",a="(a) they can send turtles the wrong way, away from the sea; (b) they can pull birds off course and leave them circling until exhausted.",g="1 mark for each of the two effects."),
 dict(d="2b",k="tf",m=1,q="Tick True or False for each statement.",items=["Skyglow can be seen for many miles.","Warmer-coloured bulbs are more harmful than bluish ones.","Light pollution disappears when the light is switched off.","Moths feed around lamps."],a=["T","F","T","F"],g=TF),
 dict(d="2g",k="short",m=2,q="In paragraph 6, the writer says light pollution disappears \u201cthe moment a light is switched off\u201d. Why does the writer compare it with a spilled chemical, and what point is made?",a="A spilled chemical stays and must be cleaned up, but light stops instantly; so light pollution is easier to fix.",g="1 mark for the contrast (chemical remains / light vanishes). 1 mark for the point that it is easy to reduce."),
 dict(d="2d",k="short",m=1,q="What effect can bluish light in the evening have on people?",a="It can make it harder to fall asleep.",g="Accept any answer about sleeping less easily."),
 dict(d="2a",k="mc",m=1,q="In paragraph 5, what does \u201cunaffected\u201d mean?",opts=["Not touched or changed","Very tired","Extremely bright","Happy"],a="A"),
 dict(d="2d",k="short",m=1,q="Why does the writer say \u201cNone of this means sitting in darkness\u201d?",a="To reassure readers that reducing light pollution is about using light carefully, not having no light.",g="Accept: it is about careful use, not removing all light."),
 dict(d="2c",k="mc",m=1,q="Which of these best describes the purpose of paragraph 6?",opts=["To describe how astronomers work","To explain what can be done to reduce light pollution","To warn about migrating birds","To describe the Milky Way"],a="B"),
 dict(d="2c",k="mc",m=1,q="Which of these is the best summary of the text?",opts=["Too much artificial light spoils the night sky and harms living things, but it can be reduced.","Streetlamps should be banned.","Only astronomers are affected by light.","Skyglow cannot be solved."],a="A"),
 dict(d="2d",k="short",m=1,q="Look at the whole text. Give one group of living things, other than astronomers, that light pollution affects, and one way it affects them.",a="Sea turtles (go the wrong way), birds (drawn off course), moths (circle lamps) or people (harder to sleep).",g="Accept any correct group with a correct effect."),
])

T(33, "33-5", "Along the Wall", "Recount (Historical)", [
"We began at Housesteads Roman Fort at nine o\u2019clock, in a fine drizzle that could not decide whether it was rain or mist. My brother Kwesi had spent the car journey complaining that a wall was only a wall. I had spent it reading aloud from the guidebook. Hadrian\u2019s Wall, I had learned, stretches about seventy-three miles across the north of England, from near Newcastle in the east to the shores of the Solway Firth in the west, and building began in AD 122, on the orders of the Emperor Hadrian.",
"\u201cSeventy-three miles,\u201d Kwesi had muttered. \u201cBy hand?\u201d",
"\u201cBy soldiers,\u201d I had said. It was not much of an answer, but we were about to discover how much it was worth.",
"Beyond the fort, the path climbed a grassy ridge, and there, quite suddenly, the wall was in front of us. It was not as tall as I had imagined. In places it rose no higher than my waist, and its grey stones were freckled with orange lichen. Yet it went on, rising and falling with the crags like the spine of some enormous sleeping animal, until the mist swallowed it. To the north, the ground dropped away in a great cliff. I could see why the builders had chosen this line: an enemy would have had to climb the whole way, in full view.",
"Every mile or so, we came to the remains of a small square building set into the wall, with a gateway through the middle. These were milecastles, where soldiers once stood guard. The guidebook said that our word \u201cmile\u201d comes from the Latin for a thousand paces, and that the soldiers had counted their steps to place each castle. I read this out. Kwesi pretended not to be listening, but I noticed that he began to count his own.",
"By late morning, the wind had scoured the mist away, and the whole country opened out below us: green hills, tiny farms, sheep dotted about like scattered rice, and a dark lake glittering under the crags. I knelt beside the wall and laid my palm against a stone. It was cold and rough, and it had been cut, shaped and hauled into place by somebody almost two thousand years ago. It was hard to picture the effort.",
"Kwesi had stopped complaining. He was standing a little way off, hands deep in his pockets, gazing north across the empty land. For once, he seemed to have nothing to say.",
"\u201cWhat would it have been like,\u201d he asked at last, \u201cto stand here and look at that, every day?\u201d",
"I could not answer. I thought of all the soldiers who must have stood on this ridge, far from home, watching for something that might never come. I thought of the wind, which surely blew as hard for them.",
"By the time we turned back, my feet ached and my hair was plastered flat with drizzle. But when I glanced behind me, the wall lay across the hills like a grey thread, stitched through the landscape by careful hands. It seemed to me that it was no longer only a wall.",
], [
 dict(d="2b",k="short",m=1,q="Look at paragraph 1. Where did the writer and Kwesi begin their walk?",a="Housesteads Roman Fort.",g="Accept: Housesteads."),
 dict(d="2b",k="short",m=1,q="According to paragraph 1, when did building begin?",a="AD 122.",g="Accept 122 / AD 122."),
 dict(d="2a",k="mc",m=1,q="In paragraph 4, what does the word \u201cfreckled\u201d suggest about the stones?",opts=["They were dotted with small patches of colour.","They were perfectly smooth.","They were brand new.","They were covered in snow."],a="A"),
 dict(d="2g",k="short",m=2,q="The wall is described as \u201cthe spine of some enormous sleeping animal\u201d. What impression does this give of the wall? Explain.",a="It suggests the wall is long, huge and rises and falls like a creature\u2019s back; it seems alive but still.",g="1 mark for size / length / shape. 1 mark for explaining the effect of a sleeping animal (still, calm, alive, powerful)."),
 dict(d="2d",k="short",m=1,q="Why does the writer think the builders chose the line of the crags?",a="An enemy would have had to climb the cliff in full view.",g="Accept any answer about the cliff making it hard to approach."),
 dict(d="2c",k="order",m=1,q="Number the events from 1 to 4 to show the order they happened in the text.",items=["The mist cleared and the view opened out.","They reached a milecastle.","They set out from the fort.","They turned back with aching feet."],a=[3,2,1,4],g=Q2),
 dict(d="2d",k="short",m=2,q="How can you tell that Kwesi\u2019s attitude changed during the walk? Give two pieces of evidence.",a="He began to count his own steps; he stopped complaining and gazed north; he asked what it would have been like to stand there.",g="1 mark for each of two pieces of evidence. Max 2."),
 dict(d="2b",k="tf",m=1,q="Tick True or False for each statement.",items=["The wall is about seventy-three miles long.","The word \u201cmile\u201d comes from the Latin for a thousand paces.","The wall was built entirely by Kwesi\u2019s ancestors.","The writer touched one of the stones."],a=["T","T","F","T"],g=TF),
 dict(d="2g",k="short",m=1,q="The sheep are \u201cdotted about like scattered rice\u201d. What does this comparison help the reader picture?",a="Small white shapes spread widely over the hills.",g="Accept any answer about small, white and scattered."),
 dict(d="2a",k="mc",m=1,q="In paragraph 6, the wind had \u201cscoured\u201d the mist away. What does \u201cscoured\u201d suggest?",opts=["Cleaned it away roughly","Painted it","Invited it","Quietly buried it"],a="A"),
 dict(d="2d",k="short",m=2,q="Look at the final paragraph. \u201cIt seemed to me that it was no longer only a wall.\u201d What does the writer now think the wall is? Use evidence to explain.",a="The wall now seems a work of people and a link to the past, because it is \u201cstitched by careful hands\u201d and the writer has pictured soldiers standing there.",g="1 mark for the idea that it stands for people / history / effort. 1 mark for evidence (soldiers on the ridge, stones hauled, stitched by careful hands)."),
 dict(d="2c",k="mc",m=1,q="What is the main purpose of paragraphs 2 and 3?",opts=["To show that Kwesi is doubtful and to set up the writer\u2019s discovery","To describe the weather","To explain how the wall was built","To give directions"],a="A"),
 dict(d="2d",k="mc",m=1,q="Which word best describes the writer\u2019s feelings by the end of the walk?",opts=["Bored","Thoughtful","Cross","Frightened"],a="B"),
 dict(d="2c",k="mc",m=1,q="Which of these best summarises the text?",opts=["A sibling argument about a guidebook","A walk along a Roman wall that changes a boy\u2019s attitude and gives the writer a sense of the past","A description of a fort","A history of the Roman Empire"],a="B"),
])

T(33, "33-6", "The Tuner", "Description", [
"The piano in the school hall had been out of tune since Christmas, and nobody had minded much until the day the tuner arrived. He was a small, neat man in a grey cardigan, and he carried a leather case that opened with two brisk clicks. Meera, who had stayed behind to stack chairs, found herself lingering at the edge of the stage.",
"He lifted the lid and unscrewed the front panel, and the piano\u2019s insides were laid bare: rows of felt hammers, a thicket of shining strings, and a scatter of dust that had not seen daylight for years. He pressed a single key and held it down. Then he leaned so close to the strings that his ear almost touched them, as though the piano were confiding a secret.",
"To Meera, the note sounded perfectly all right. She said so.",
"\u201cListen again,\u201d said the tuner, without turning his head. \u201cNot to the beginning of the note. To the middle of it.\u201d",
"She listened. Beneath the sound, so faint she might have imagined it, was a slow wobble, a soft wah, wah, wah, like a pulse.",
"\u201cThere are two or three strings for each note,\u201d he explained. \u201cIf they are even slightly different, they quarrel. That is what you can hear. My job is to persuade them to agree.\u201d",
"He fitted a small steel lever to a peg and turned it a hair\u2019s breadth. The wobble slowed, stretched and, quite suddenly, stopped. The note that remained was clean and round, like water in a clear glass. Meera realised that she had been listening to a smudged note all term, and had not known it.",
"For an hour, the tuner worked his way up the keyboard, one note at a time. He struck each of them again and again, humming under his breath, adjusting, testing. The hall filled with a strange, patient music that was not really music at all. Rain tapped on the high windows. Meera opened her homework in the doorway, though she kept forgetting to read it.",
"When at last he finished, he sat down and played a slow chord. It rose into the rafters, glowing, and hung there. Meera found that her throat had tightened, though she could not have said why.",
"\u201cHas it always sounded like that?\u201d she asked.",
"\u201cNot always,\u201d the tuner said, closing the lid. \u201cIt will drift again. Strings stretch and the weather changes. Nothing stays in tune for ever. That is why people like me have a job.\u201d",
"After he had gone, the hall seemed larger and emptier. Meera lifted the lid, pressed the middle key, and did as she had been told: she listened not to the beginning of the note, but to the middle of it. Nothing wobbled. She kept on listening until the sound had faded to nothing, and even then she stayed a little while longer.",
], [
 dict(d="2b",k="short",m=1,q="What was the tuner wearing?",a="A grey cardigan.",g="Accept: grey cardigan."),
 dict(d="2a",k="mc",m=1,q="In paragraph 1, what does \u201clingering\u201d mean?",opts=["Leaving quickly","Shouting","Staying somewhere a little longer","Dancing"],a="C"),
 dict(d="2b",k="find",m=1,q="Find and copy the words from paragraph 2 that compare the strings to something in a forest or garden.",a="a thicket of shining strings",g="Accept \u201ca thicket\u201d."),
 dict(d="2g",k="short",m=2,q="The tuner leaned close \u201cas though the piano were confiding a secret\u201d. What does this suggest about how he treats the piano? Explain.",a="He treats it like a living thing that speaks to him quietly, listening with great care and respect.",g="1 mark for treating the piano as living / listening carefully. 1 mark for explaining the idea of a secret being told quietly."),
 dict(d="2d",k="short",m=1,q="Why did Meera think the note sounded \u201cperfectly all right\u201d at first?",a="She could not hear the faint wobble in the middle of the note.",g="Accept: she did not know how to listen / she was not listening to the middle."),
 dict(d="2c",k="order",m=1,q="Number the events from 1 to 4 to show the order they happened.",items=["The wobble stopped.","The tuner explained why the strings quarrel.","Meera said the note sounded fine.","The tuner played a slow chord."],a=[3,2,1,4],g=Q2),
 dict(d="2d",k="short",m=2,q="What did the tuner mean when he said the strings \u201cquarrel\u201d? Explain what he was describing.",a="The strings on the same note were slightly different in pitch, so the sounds clashed and caused the wobble.",g="1 mark for strings being slightly different. 1 mark for the wobble / clash as the result."),
 dict(d="2b",k="tf",m=1,q="Tick True or False for each statement.",items=["The tuner worked for about an hour.","The piano had been out of tune since Christmas.","The tuner said a piano stays in tune for ever.","Meera left before the tuner had finished."],a=["T","T","F","F"],g=TF),
 dict(d="2g",k="short",m=1,q="The note is described as \u201clike water in a clear glass\u201d. What does this suggest?",a="That it was clean, pure and clear.",g="Accept: clear / pure / clean."),
 dict(d="2d",k="short",m=2,q="Why do you think Meera\u2019s \u201cthroat had tightened\u201d when she heard the chord? Use evidence from the text.",a="The music was so beautiful and moving that it made her emotional; the sound \u201crose into the rafters, glowing\u201d.",g="1 mark for a feeling (moved / emotional / amazed). 1 mark for evidence from paragraph 9 or from the contrast with the smudged note."),
 dict(d="2a",k="mc",m=1,q="In paragraph 11, what does \u201cdrift\u201d mean?",opts=["Become louder","Slowly move away from the correct state","Be sold","Be stolen"],a="B"),
 dict(d="2d",k="mc",m=1,q="What does the ending suggest about Meera?",opts=["She wants to become a tuner tomorrow.","She is bored.","She dislikes the hall.","She has learned to listen more carefully and appreciate the sound."],a="D"),
 dict(d="2c",k="mc",m=1,q="Which of these is the best summary of the text?",opts=["A piano is broken beyond repair.","A visit from a piano tuner teaches a pupil to listen closely.","Two children argue about music.","A hall is cleaned."],a="B"),
 dict(d="2e",k="short",m=1,q="What might Meera do next time she hears a piano? Explain using the text.",a="Listen to the middle of the note, because the tuner taught her how.",g="Accept any sensible prediction linked to listening carefully."),
])
